Gremlins, Inc. reminds me a lot of the place I was born in: Brazil... where, sadly, I still live.
In this game:
You must step on others to achieve victory (that's the rule around here);
You gotta pay a ton of taxes if you wanna get anywhere fast (in this s**thole of a country you actually pay a lot more than that, and things are still as slow as they could possibly be... that's when the bribes come in!);
Every time you run into the police, they'll ask you for a bribe (here, in Brazil, RNG also gives you the chance of a beating, torture, or murder);
If you fool enough ret**ds, buy enough votes, or make some passionate, although completely idiotic, speech you'll end up becoming the Governor (here we also call them "President," "Senator," etc.);
If you are the Governor, you can freely get all the bribes other players pay to enrich yourself, and you don't have to pay any taxes/bribes yourself (Rio de Janeiro's governor got arrested recently for stealing about half a billion dolars in bribes);
When you get sent to jail, you can keep stealing from other players from the comfort of your cell (all "organized" [if you can call them that] crime leaders here are either free, or giving orders from within jail with the most recent iPhone);
You can lie to Justice, and send other players to jail (in real life, usually when you are a woman... you know, the old "he r*ped me!" or "he beat me!" to get some money out of it);
You can tell the truth to Justice, and send other players to jail (biggest corruption scandal in the history of mankind is currently unveiling here thanks, mainly, to this. And we are proud of it!);
You can rob a bank, dump waste into the water, etc., with a good chance to get away with it (some fellows here have been exploding banks in the middle of the night for years now, and not one got caught yet. Funny thing is: half the time they blow half the money up as well.)
The list goes on but, seriously, if you play this you'll get a decent idea of what it is like to live in the third world. The only difference is that in Gremlins, Inc. you can still go to jail if you're the Governor. In real life, it's nearly impossible.
So, yeah... real life sucks. Hard.
But the game, Gremlins, Inc., is totally awesome.

